# DOL (Direct-On-Line) Starter Ladder Logic

## Overview
This ladder logic implements a standard DOL starter for three-phase induction motor control with basic protection features.

## Network 1: Main Control Circuit (Start/Stop with Latching)
```
      Start PB          Stop PB (NC)       Overload          Contactor
|----[ ]--------------[ / ]-------------[ / ]-------------( )----|
|        |
|        |---------[ Auxiliary Contact ]----------------------|
|                   (Normally Open)
```

## Network 2: Motor Status Indication
```
      Contactor Status          Running Indicator
|----[ ]------------------------------------------( )----|
```

## Network 3: Fault Indication
```
      Overload Trip          Fault Indicator
|----[ ]------------------------------------------( )----|
```

## Network 4: Ready Status
```
      Stop PB (NC)           Overload (NC)       Ready Indicator
|----[ / ]------------------[ / ]-----------------( )----|
```

## Detailed Logic Description

### Network 1: Main Control Circuit
- **Start PB**: Normally Open push button (momentary contact)
- **Stop PB**: Normally Closed push button (safety - opens on press)
- **Overload**: Normally Closed contact from overload relay (opens on trip)
- **Contactor**: Output coil that controls the main contactor
- **Auxiliary Contact**: Latching contact from main contactor (NO)

**Operation:**
1. Pressing Start PB energizes the contactor coil
2. Contactor closes and auxiliary contact provides latching
3. Releasing Start PB maintains contactor through latching
4. Pressing Stop PB breaks the circuit, de-energizing contactor
5. Overload trip breaks the circuit for motor protection

### Network 2: Motor Status Indication
- **Contactor Status**: Feedback from auxiliary contact
- **Running Indicator**: Output to HMI/panel indicator

**Operation:**
- Illuminates when motor is running
- Provides visual confirmation of motor operation

### Network 3: Fault Indication
- **Overload Trip**: Normally Open contact from overload relay
- **Fault Indicator**: Output to fault alarm/HMI

**Operation:**
- Activates when overload relay trips
- Provides fault notification to operator

### Network 4: Ready Status
- **Stop PB (NC)**: Inverted stop button signal
- **Overload (NC)**: Inverted overload signal
- **Ready Indicator**: Output to ready status light/HMI

**Operation:**
- Illuminates when system is ready to start
- Requires stop button not pressed and no overload condition

## I/O Tag Assignment

### Inputs
| Tag Name | Address | Data Type | Description |
|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|
| DI_DOL_Start_PB | %I0.0 | BOOL | DOL Start push button |
| DI_DOL_Stop_PB | %I0.1 | BOOL | DOL Stop push button |
| DI_DOL_Overload | %I0.2 | BOOL | Overload relay contact |
| DI_DOL_Aux_Contact | %I0.3 | BOOL | Contactor auxiliary feedback |

### Outputs
| Tag Name | Address | Data Type | Description |
|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|
| DQ_DOL_Contactor | %Q0.0 | BOOL | Contactor coil output |
| DQ_DOL_Running_Ind | %Q0.1 | BOOL | Motor running indicator |
| DQ_DOL_Fault_Ind | %Q0.2 | BOOL | Fault indicator |
| DQ_DOL_Ready_Ind | %Q0.3 | BOOL | Ready status indicator |

## Safety Features
1. **Stop Button Priority**: Normally closed for fail-safe operation
2. **Overload Protection**: Automatically trips on motor overload
3. **Latching Safety**: Requires positive action to start motor
4. **Status Indication**: Clear visual feedback of system state

## Typical Applications
- Small to medium power induction motors (up to 7.5 kW)
- Pump drives
- Fan controls
- Conveyor systems
- Compressor controls

## Advantages
- Simple and reliable
- Low cost
- Easy to troubleshoot
- Minimal components
- Direct-on-line starting (simplest starting method)

## Limitations
- High starting current (6-7 times rated current)
- Mechanical stress during starting
- Limited to applications where high starting current is acceptable
- Not suitable for large motors or applications requiring soft start

## Commissioning Checklist
- [ ] Verify all wiring connections
- [ ] Test emergency stop functionality
- [ ] Verify overload relay setting
- [ ] Check auxiliary contact operation
- [ ] Test start/stop sequence
- [ ] Verify indicator functionality
- [ ] Document all settings
- [ ] Perform load test

## Troubleshooting Guide
| Symptom | Possible Cause | Action |
|---------|----------------|--------|
| Motor won't start | Stop button pressed | Release stop button |
| Motor won't start | Overload tripped | Reset overload, check motor |
| Motor won't start | No control power | Check control circuit fuse |
| Motor starts but won't latch | Auxiliary contact faulty | Replace contactor |
| Contactor chattering | Low control voltage | Check control supply |
| Frequent overload trips | Motor overloaded | Reduce load, check motor |

## Standards Compliance
- IEC 60204-1 (Safety of machinery)
- IEC 60947 (Low-voltage switchgear)
- NFPA 79 (Industrial Machinery)
- Local electrical codes and regulations

## Maintenance Requirements
- Monthly: Check contactor contacts for wear
- Monthly: Verify tightness of electrical connections
- Quarterly: Test overload relay operation
- Annually: Complete insulation resistance test
- As needed: Replace worn contacts and components
